// ===== WHY IT WORKS: body science, God's design, scripture to meditate on and an action, for every practice =====
// code: [why it works in the body, how God designed it, 'Ref|verse text', 'more verses to meditate on', 'take action today']
const PDESIGN={
 sigh:['Two inhales re-open collapsed air sacs in the lungs, then the long exhale slows the heart through the vagus nerve. In a 2023 Stanford study, five minutes a day of cyclic sighing lowered anxiety more than mindfulness meditation.','God built a calming switch into every breath: the out-breath is wired to the "rest" branch of the nervous system. You carry His peace-making design with you everywhere.','Genesis 2:7|The LORD God formed man from the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life.','Job 33:4; Psalm 150:6; John 20:22','Before your next hard conversation, take two physiological sighs and whisper "Your breath is in me."'],
 box:['Equal counts give the mind a simple rhythm to follow, which pulls attention away from threat and steadies breathing and heart rate. It is used by first responders because it works under pressure.','Rhythm is part of creation: day and night, seasons, heartbeat. A steady rhythm tells the body the world is ordered, not chaotic.','Psalm 46:10|Be still, and know that I am God.','Ecclesiastes 3:1; Isaiah 26:3; Psalm 4:8','Use one round of box breathing at a red light or before opening your email.'],
 exhale:['When you breathe out, the heart naturally slows (respiratory sinus arrhythmia). Making the exhale longer than the inhale tips the balance toward the parasympathetic "brake".','The body was designed to recover, not to stay on alert. The long exhale is a built-in way of letting go, a physical picture of casting your cares.','1 Peter 5:7|Cast all your worries on him, because he cares for you.','Psalm 62:5; Matthew 11:28; Philippians 4:6-7','Each time you breathe out slowly today, name one worry and hand it over.'],
 ground:['Naming things you can see, hear and feel moves activity toward the sensory and thinking parts of the brain and away from the alarm. It anchors you in the present, where you are actually safe.','God made us embodied creatures who meet Him in the here and now. The present moment is where His help is found.','Psalm 118:24|This is the day that the LORD has made. We will rejoice and be glad in it.','Matthew 6:34; Lamentations 3:22-23; Psalm 16:8','When memories or worries pull you away, name three things God has put around you right now.'],
 orient:['Slowly turning your head and eyes lets the brainstem scan for danger and find none. Your nervous system relaxes when it sees for itself that the room is safe.','Like a lamb looking around the fold, we are made to look for safety, and God invites us to find it in Him.','Psalm 121:1-2|I will lift up my eyes to the hills. Where does my help come from? My help comes from the LORD.','Psalm 23:4; Psalm 91:1-2; Isaiah 41:10','Arriving somewhere new today, pause and look slowly around before you speak.'],
 tipp:['Cold water on the face triggers the dive reflex, which slows the heart within seconds. Intense movement burns off stress hormones, and paced breathing and muscle release finish the job.','The body has emergency brakes for overwhelming moments. Using them is good stewardship, not weakness.','Psalm 18:6|In my distress I called on the LORD, and cried to my God. He heard my voice.','Psalm 61:2; 2 Corinthians 4:8-9; Isaiah 43:2','Put a cold pack or bottle of water in the fridge now, so it is ready for your next flood moment.'],
 label:['Putting a feeling into words engages the prefrontal cortex and turns down amygdala activity (affect labelling, Lieberman 2007). Naming is the first step of taming.','God gave Adam the work of naming (Gen 2:19), and the Psalms name every emotion honestly before God. Naming is an act of dominion and honesty.','Psalm 62:8|Pour out your heart before him. God is a refuge for us.','Psalm 42:5; Psalm 139:23; Romans 8:26','Three times today, finish the sentence "Right now I feel…, because…"'],
 defuse:['Saying "I\'m having the thought that…" creates distance between you and the thought, so it loses some of its grip (ACT research). Thoughts become events you notice, not commands.','We are called to take thoughts captive, not to be taken captive by them. Not every thought that passes through your mind is true, or yours to obey.','2 Corinthians 10:5|We take every thought captive to the obedience of Christ.','Philippians 4:8; Romans 12:2; Proverbs 4:23','Catch one harsh thought today and add "I\'m having the thought that…" before it.'],
 surf:['Urges rise, peak and fall like a wave, usually within 20–30 minutes. Each time you ride one out without acting, the brain learns the urge can pass, and it weakens.','Self-control is fruit the Spirit grows in us (Gal 5:22-23), not willpower we manufacture. Waiting with God through the wave is how that fruit grows.','1 Corinthians 10:13|God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted above what you are able, but will with the temptation also make the way of escape.','Galatians 5:22-23; Hebrews 4:15-16; James 1:12','When the next urge comes, set a 15-minute timer, pray, and watch the wave instead of obeying it.'],
 stop:['A one-second pause lets the slower prefrontal cortex catch up with the fast alarm. That gap is where choice lives.','Scripture repeatedly calls us to be slow to anger and quick to listen. The pause is a doorway for wisdom.','James 1:19|Let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, and slow to anger.','Proverbs 15:1; Proverbs 16:32; Ephesians 4:26','Pick one trigger you know is coming today and decide now: Stop, Take a breath, Observe, Proceed.'],
 scb:['Self-compassion activates the soothing system (oxytocin, vagal calm) instead of the threat system. People who treat themselves kindly recover from failure faster and try again more often (Neff; Gilbert).','God\'s posture toward you is compassion. Treating yourself as He treats you is agreement with Him, not self-indulgence.','Psalm 103:13-14|Like a father has compassion on his children, so the LORD has compassion on those who fear him. For he knows how we are made.','Isaiah 49:15; Lamentations 3:22; Romans 8:1','When you fail today, put a hand on your heart and say: "This is hard. Others struggle too. Lord, be kind to me, and help me be kind to myself."'],
 opp:['Emotions push for actions. Doing the opposite (approaching instead of avoiding, softening instead of attacking) sends new information to the brain and changes the emotion itself (DBT).','The Spirit leads us into responses our flesh would not choose: blessing those who curse, approaching instead of hiding. Opposite action is walking in that new direction.','Romans 12:21|Don\'t be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good.','Luke 6:27-28; Galatians 5:16; Philippians 2:3-4','Choose one urge today (to hide, snap or quit) and do the opposite on purpose.'],
 start2:['Starting is the hardest part. Once you begin, the brain\'s reward system releases dopamine as you make progress, which makes it easier to continue (the Zeigarnik and progress effects).','God works through small beginnings and faithful steps, not only through big leaps.','Zechariah 4:10|For who has despised the day of small things?','Luke 16:10; Proverbs 13:4; Colossians 3:23','Pick the task you are avoiding and do only the first two minutes, now.'],
 dump:['Unfinished tasks keep looping in working memory. Writing them down tells the brain they are safely stored, which frees attention and helps sleep (Scullin 2018: to-do lists before bed helped people fall asleep faster).','We are invited to bring everything to God in prayer, so our minds can be guarded by His peace.','Philippians 4:6-7|In everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God. And the peace of God will guard your hearts and your thoughts.','Psalm 55:22; Proverbs 16:3; Matthew 6:33','Tonight, write every open loop on one page, then pray over the page and close the notebook.'],
 shift:['Different states need different tools: calming tools lower arousal, energising tools raise it. Matching the tool to the state is more effective than using one tool for everything.','God made us with seasons and states, and Scripture offers different responses for each: rest for the weary, courage for the fearful, comfort for the grieving.','Ecclesiastes 3:1,4|For everything there is a season… a time to weep, and a time to laugh.','Isaiah 40:29-31; Psalm 30:5; Romans 12:15','Check your state at lunch today and pick one tool that matches it.'],
 reset:['Movement, daylight, water and a short break increase blood flow and alertness and clear "attention residue". The brain needs pauses to consolidate and refocus.','Even Jesus withdrew to quiet places. Pausing is part of a good design, not a failure of productivity.','Mark 6:31|Come away into a deserted place, and rest awhile.','Psalm 23:2-3; Isaiah 30:15; Matthew 11:28-30','Take a ten-minute walk outside this afternoon, with no phone in your hand.'],
 contain:['Imagining a secure container gives the mind permission to set distressing material aside until there is a safe time to process it. It is widely used in trauma therapy for stabilisation.','God holds what we cannot carry right now. Setting something down in His keeping is trust, not avoidance.','Psalm 56:8|You count my wanderings. You put my tears into your bottle. Aren\'t they in your book?','Psalm 31:5; 2 Timothy 1:12; Isaiah 46:4','Picture one heavy memory going into a locked box in God\'s hands, to open later with a safe person.'],
 reach:['Being with a safe person lowers stress hormones and calms the alarm (co-regulation). Social support is one of the strongest predictors of resilience.','"It is not good for the man to be alone." We are designed for connection; asking for help is how the body of Christ is meant to work.','Galatians 6:2|Bear one another\'s burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ.','Ecclesiastes 4:9-10; Hebrews 10:24-25; Romans 12:15','Send one honest message today: "I\'m finding things hard. Could we talk this week?"'],
 pmr:['Tensing then releasing muscles teaches the body the difference between tension and relaxation, and reduces anxiety, insomnia and tension pain.','The body holds what the heart carries. Releasing it on purpose is a way of honouring the temple you were given.','Psalm 4:8|In peace I will both lay myself down and sleep, for you, LORD alone, make me live in safety.','1 Corinthians 6:19-20; Psalm 127:2; Matthew 11:28','Do a five-minute muscle release tonight, starting with your jaw and shoulders.'],
 scan:['Moving attention slowly through the body strengthens interoception (the insula\'s reading of body signals), which improves emotional awareness and lowers reactivity.','You are fearfully and wonderfully made. Paying kind attention to your body is a way of receiving it as a gift.','Psalm 139:14|I will give thanks to you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.','1 Thessalonians 5:23; Romans 12:1; Psalm 139:13','Before sleep, scan from head to toe and thank God for one part of your body.'],
 record:['Writing down a thought and checking the evidence engages the prefrontal cortex to re-evaluate the alarm. Over time, balanced thoughts become more automatic (CBT is one of the best-evidenced therapies for anxiety and depression).','Truth sets us free. Testing thoughts against reality and God\'s word is renewing the mind.','Romans 12:2|Don\'t be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind.','John 8:32; Philippians 4:8; Psalm 119:105','Write one thought record today about the moment that upset you most.'],
 worry:['Postponing worry to a set time shrinks the hours it takes over and teaches the brain that worries can wait. It reduces worry in controlled studies.','Jesus does not shame worry; He invites us to bring tomorrow\'s troubles to the Father and live today.','Matthew 6:34|Don\'t be anxious for tomorrow, for tomorrow will be anxious for itself. Each day\'s own evil is sufficient.','Matthew 6:25-33; Philippians 4:6; 1 Peter 5:7','Set a 15-minute worry-and-prayer time for this evening, and park worries until then.'],
 ba:['Low mood shrinks activity, and low activity lowers mood further. Scheduling small, meaningful activities restarts the brain\'s reward system; behavioural activation works as well as CBT for depression.','God created us to work, play and connect. Small steps of faithfulness bring life even when feelings lag behind.','Isaiah 40:31|Those who wait for the LORD will renew their strength.','Philippians 4:13; Psalm 37:3; Galatians 6:9','Schedule one small enjoyable thing and one small useful thing for tomorrow.'],
 ifthen:['If-then plans ("When X happens, I will Y") hand the decision to an automatic cue, which roughly doubles follow-through in studies (Gollwitzer).','Wisdom plans ahead. Deciding in advance is how we guard our hearts when the moment comes.','Proverbs 16:9|A man\'s heart plans his course, but the LORD directs his steps.','Proverbs 21:5; Psalm 119:11; Daniel 1:8','Write one if-then plan for the moment of the day you most often slip.'],
 deepw:['Single-tasking avoids the cost of switching, which can eat up to 40% of productive time. Focus improves with practice, like a muscle.','Work done wholeheartedly is worship. Undivided attention is a gift you give God and the people you serve.','Colossians 3:23|Whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men.','Proverbs 4:25-27; Ecclesiastes 9:10; Luke 10:41-42','Block one 60-minute focus session tomorrow, phone in another room.'],
 pomo:['Short timed sprints make starting easier and protect focus; the built-in breaks prevent fatigue.','Rhythms of work and rest run through creation. Even small work periods with rest mirror the Sabbath pattern.','Exodus 20:9-10|You shall labour six days, and do all your work, but the seventh day is a Sabbath to the LORD.','Psalm 90:12; Ecclesiastes 3:1; Mark 6:31','Try three 25-minute sprints today, each with a real five-minute break.'],
 flow:['Flow happens when the challenge is just above your skill, goals are clear and feedback is fast. Setting these up makes deep, satisfying focus more likely.','God gifted people with skill and joy in their craft, like Bezalel. Flow is a taste of the joy of doing what you were made for.','Exodus 31:3|I have filled him with the Spirit of God, in wisdom, and in understanding, and in knowledge, and in all kinds of workmanship.','Psalm 90:17; Ecclesiastes 3:22; 1 Peter 4:10','Choose one task you enjoy and set a clear goal and time limit for it today.'],
 sleep:['A regular wind-down lowers light, arousal and core temperature, which helps melatonin rise. Consistent sleep times strengthen the body clock, mood and memory.','Sleep is a daily act of trust: God keeps watch while you are not in control.','Psalm 127:2|He gives to his beloved sleep.','Psalm 4:8; Psalm 121:3-4; Proverbs 3:24','Set an alarm one hour before bed tonight to start winding down.'],
 safe:['Imagining a safe place in detail activates the same calming circuits as being there, and builds a resource you can return to under stress.','God Himself is our refuge and dwelling place. Imagining safety in His presence is meditation on a real truth.','Psalm 91:1|He who dwells in the secret place of the Most High will rest in the shadow of the Almighty.','Psalm 23; Psalm 46:1; Proverbs 18:10','Spend five minutes picturing yourself resting with Jesus in a place of peace.'],
 glim:['The brain has a negativity bias. Pausing to notice small moments of safety and joy for 10–20 seconds helps them register and slowly retrains attention (Hanson; Dana).','Gratitude is commanded because it is good for us: it trains the eye to see God\'s gifts that are already there.','Psalm 103:2|Praise the LORD, my soul, and don\'t forget all his benefits.','1 Thessalonians 5:18; James 1:17; Philippians 4:8','Write down three glimmers before bed and thank God for each.'],
 ba2:['Movement burns off stress hormones, raises endorphins and BDNF (a brain growth factor), and is as effective as medication for mild to moderate depression in many studies.','Our bodies were made to move, walk and work. Physical care is spiritual care for the whole person.','1 Timothy 4:8|For bodily exercise has some value, but godliness has value for all things.','1 Corinthians 6:19-20; Isaiah 40:31; 3 John 1:2','Take a 20-minute brisk walk today and pray as you go.'],
 solve:['Breaking a problem into steps moves it from the alarm system to the planning brain, reducing overwhelm and making action possible.','God gives wisdom generously to those who ask. Problem-solving with prayer combines trust and responsibility.','James 1:5|If any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all generously.','Proverbs 3:5-6; Proverbs 15:22; Nehemiah 4:9','Write down one problem, three possible solutions, and the first step of one.'],
 ladder:['Facing fear in small graded steps teaches the amygdala that the feared thing is survivable (exposure therapy, the gold-standard treatment for anxiety).','"Do not fear" is repeated throughout Scripture, always with "for I am with you". Courage grows step by step in His company.','Joshua 1:9|Be strong and courageous. Don\'t be afraid, for the LORD your God is with you wherever you go.','Isaiah 41:10; Psalm 27:1; 2 Timothy 1:7','Take the next small step on your fear ladder today, and tell someone you did it.'],
 letter:['Writing to yourself from a compassionate perspective calms the threat system and reduces shame and self-criticism (Gilbert\'s compassion-focused therapy).','You can write to yourself what God says over you. His words are kinder than your inner critic.','Zephaniah 3:17|The LORD your God is among you… He will rejoice over you with joy. He will calm you in his love.','Isaiah 43:1-4; Jeremiah 31:3; 1 John 3:1','Write a short letter to yourself in God\'s voice, using one of these verses.'],
 critic:['Naming the inner critic and answering it with balanced, kind words weakens a well-worn neural pathway and builds a new one.','The accuser\'s voice condemns; the Spirit\'s voice convicts gently and restores. Learning to tell them apart is spiritual discernment.','Romans 8:1|There is therefore now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus.','Revelation 12:10; Romans 8:33-34; 1 John 3:20','When the critic speaks today, ask: "Is this conviction that leads to life, or condemnation that leads to shame?"'],
 dearman:['A clear structure keeps you regulated while asking, and makes it more likely the other person hears you without feeling attacked (DBT).','Speaking the truth in love is the biblical pattern: honest about needs, kind in tone.','Ephesians 4:15|Speaking truth in love, we may grow up in all things into him who is the head, Christ.','Proverbs 25:11; Colossians 4:6; Matthew 5:37','Write one DEAR MAN request you have been avoiding, and choose a time to say it.'],
 bscript:['Clear, calm limits reduce resentment and burnout and protect relationships over time. Saying no gets easier with practice as the threat response weakens.','Even Jesus had limits: He withdrew, said no, and let people walk away. A yes to God sometimes means a no to people.','Matthew 5:37|But let your "Yes" be "Yes" and your "No" be "No".','Galatians 6:5; Proverbs 4:23; Mark 1:35-38','Say one small, kind no this week without over-explaining.'],
 repair:['Repair after conflict is the strongest predictor of lasting relationships (Gottman). Each repair teaches both nervous systems that closeness survives conflict.','Reconciliation is at the heart of the gospel. Every repair is a small picture of what God has done for us.','Matthew 5:23-24|First be reconciled to your brother, and then come and offer your gift.','2 Corinthians 5:18; Ephesians 4:32; Colossians 3:13','Go back to one recent conflict and say: "I\'m sorry for my part. Can we try again?"'],
 swap:['Habits run on cue, routine and reward. Keeping the cue and reward but swapping the routine is the most reliable way to change a habit (Duhigg; habit research).','Scripture speaks of putting off the old and putting on the new: replacement, not only removal.','Ephesians 4:22-24|Put away… the old man… and put on the new man, who in the likeness of God has been created in righteousness.','Colossians 3:9-10; Galatians 5:16; Romans 6:13','Name one habit, its cue and its reward, and choose a new routine for the same cue.'],
 reparent:['Imagining giving your younger self the care they needed can soften old emotional memories (similar to schema therapy and memory reconsolidation work).','God is a Father to the fatherless. He meets the child in you with the care that was missing.','Psalm 27:10|When my father and my mother forsake me, then the LORD will take me up.','Psalm 68:5; Isaiah 66:13; Mark 10:14-16','Picture your younger self in a hard memory and imagine Jesus sitting with them. What does He say?'],
 lament:['Expressing pain in words, then turning toward hope, reduces distress more than suppressing it (expressive writing research, Pennebaker).','A third of the Psalms are laments. God invites honest complaint inside relationship, not polite silence.','Psalm 13:1,5|How long, LORD? Will you forget me forever?… But I trust in your loving kindness.','Psalm 22; Psalm 88; Lamentations 3:19-24','Write your own four-line lament: address God, complain honestly, ask, then trust.'],
 values:['Acting on chosen values, even when feelings disagree, increases meaning and reduces depression and anxiety (ACT).','God\'s call gives direction to our days. Values are a way of naming what love looks like in each area of life.','Micah 6:8|What does the LORD require of you, but to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God?','Matthew 22:37-39; Colossians 3:17; Psalm 90:12','Take one five-minute action today toward the value you\'ve neglected most.'],
 review:['Reviewing progress weekly consolidates learning and boosts motivation; seeing small wins releases dopamine and builds momentum.','God told Israel to remember and to set up stones of remembrance. Looking back on His faithfulness builds faith for the week ahead.','Lamentations 3:22-23|It is because of the LORD\'s loving kindnesses that we are not consumed… They are new every morning. Great is your faithfulness.','Joshua 4:6-7; Psalm 77:11; Psalm 103:2','This weekend, write three things that went well and one thing you will try differently.'],
 forgive:['Forgiveness lowers rumination, blood pressure and stress hormones. It is a process over time, not a single feeling.','We forgive because we have been forgiven. Releasing the debt to God is trust in His justice, not denial of the wrong.','Colossians 3:13|Bearing with one another, and forgiving each other… even as Christ forgave you, so you also do.','Ephesians 4:31-32; Romans 12:19; Matthew 18:21-35','Name one debt you are still holding, and tell God: "I hand this to You. You are the judge."'],
 story:['Putting your life into a coherent story is linked with healing from trauma and with secure attachment (Siegel; narrative therapy).','God is the author of your story, and He weaves even painful chapters into good (Gen 50:20).','Genesis 50:20|You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good.','Romans 8:28; Psalm 139:16; Philippians 1:6','Write the titles of your life\'s chapters so far, and where you see God in each.'],
 voo:['Humming and long "voo" sounds vibrate the throat and lengthen the exhale, stimulating the vagus nerve and calming the heart.','God\'s people have always sung and hummed in hard times. Singing is a gift that calms the body and lifts the spirit.','Psalm 42:8|In the night his song shall be with me.','Ephesians 5:19; Acts 16:25; Psalm 59:16','Hum a hymn or worship song slowly for two minutes when you feel tense.'],
 hug:['Gentle self-touch releases oxytocin and lowers cortisol. The alternating taps of the butterfly hug are used in EMDR to calm distress.','God made touch to communicate safety. Holding yourself kindly echoes the way He holds you.','Deuteronomy 33:27|The eternal God is your dwelling place. Underneath are the everlasting arms.','Isaiah 40:11; Isaiah 66:13; Psalm 63:8','When distress rises, cross your arms, tap slowly and pray: "Underneath are Your everlasting arms."'],
 rhythm:['Repetitive rhythms (walking, rocking, drumming) regulate the brainstem, which is why they calm babies and adults alike (Perry\'s neurosequential model).','Creation is full of rhythm: heartbeat, breath, tides, seasons. Rhythm brings the body back into God\'s ordered design.','Genesis 8:22|While the earth remains, seedtime and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter, and day and night will not cease.','Psalm 104:19-23; Ecclesiastes 3:1-8; Psalm 42:8','Take a slow rhythmic walk today, matching your steps to a short prayer.'],
 sensory:['Reducing noise, light and clutter lowers the load on an overwhelmed nervous system; changing a sense (cold, scent, texture) can interrupt a stress spiral.','God led Elijah to a quiet place and spoke in a still, small voice. Quiet is often where restoration begins.','1 Kings 19:12|After the fire, there was a still small voice.','Psalm 23:2; Isaiah 30:15; Mark 1:35','Create one quiet corner at home with soft light and no screens.'],
 pend:['Swinging attention between a resource and a little discomfort teaches the nervous system it can return to calm, widening the window of tolerance (Somatic Experiencing).','God walks with us through the valley, not around it, and keeps bringing us back to green pastures.','Psalm 23:4|Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me.','Psalm 30:5; Isaiah 43:2; 2 Corinthians 4:16-17','Practise pendulation for five minutes: touch the hard feeling, then return to a safe body place, three times.'],
 regmap:['Knowing your own warning signs and best tools makes it easier to act early, before stress becomes overwhelm.','Self-knowledge is part of wisdom. Knowing how you are made helps you steward the life God gave you.','Proverbs 14:8|The wisdom of the prudent is to think about his way.','Psalm 139:23-24; 2 Corinthians 13:5; Proverbs 4:26','Write your three warning signs and three best tools on one card and keep it with you.'],
 halt:['Hunger, anger, loneliness and tiredness all lower the brain\'s self-control resources. Meeting the basic need first makes the urge smaller.','God met Elijah\'s exhaustion with food and sleep before anything else. Basic needs are not unspiritual.','1 Kings 19:5|Behold, an angel touched him, and said to him, "Arise and eat!"','Mark 6:31; Matthew 11:28; Psalm 127:2','Before your next big decision today, check HALT and meet one need first.'],
 cope:['Rehearsing a hard situation in your mind activates many of the same brain circuits as doing it, so your skills come more easily in the moment.','Jesus prepared His disciples for what was coming. Preparing ahead is wise love for your future self.','John 16:33|In the world you have trouble; but cheer up! I have overcome the world.','Proverbs 22:3; Luke 14:28; Nehemiah 4:9','Rehearse tomorrow\'s hardest moment for five minutes, picturing yourself calm and God with you.'],
 parts:['Approaching inner conflict with curiosity instead of fighting it lowers inner tension and lets protective parts relax (Internal Family Systems).','God searches and knows every part of us, and invites even our fearful or angry parts to come to Him.','Psalm 86:11|Teach me your way, LORD. I will walk in your truth. Make my heart undivided to fear your name.','Psalm 139:1-4; Romans 7:15-25; Hebrews 4:15-16','Ask one reacting part today: "What are you afraid would happen if you stopped?"'],
 helpcheck:['Pausing to check your motive and the long-term effect prevents burnout and resentment and helps the other person grow.','Love carries others\' burdens and lets each carry their own load. Both are part of God\'s design for healthy community.','Galatians 6:2,5|Bear one another\'s burdens… For each man will bear his own burden.','Philippians 2:4; Proverbs 3:27; 1 Corinthians 13:5','Before stepping in this week, ask: "Do you want ideas, help, or a listener?"'],
 carecheck:['Noticing fear behind control lets you choose honesty instead of pressure, which protects trust and connection.','Love does not insist on its own way. God gives real freedom and still keeps loving.','1 Corinthians 13:4-5|Love is patient and is kind… doesn\'t seek its own way.','Luke 15:11-24; 1 John 4:18; Galatians 5:1','Replace one pressuring sentence this week with "I\'m scared, and it\'s your choice."'],
 gutbreath:['Belly breathing moves the diaphragm, massages the gut and increases vagal activity, which switches digestion back on.','The body was designed to digest in peace. Breathing slowly is a way of agreeing with that design.','Isaiah 30:15|In returning and rest you will be saved. In quietness and in confidence will be your strength.','Psalm 131:2; Philippians 4:6-7; Matthew 11:28-29','Before each meal today, take six slow belly breaths.'],
 restdigest:['A calm, slow meal starts the brain\'s "cephalic phase" of digestion, so enzymes and gut movement work as they should.','Jesus often healed and restored people around tables. Eating with thanks and company is a gift, not a race.','Acts 2:46|They took their food with gladness and singleness of heart.','1 Timothy 4:4-5; Ecclesiastes 9:7; Luke 24:30-31','Eat one meal today sitting down, giving thanks first and putting your fork down between bites.'],
 gutlog:['Tracking symptoms with stress, sleep and food reveals personal patterns, which helps you and your doctor choose what to change.','Wisdom pays attention. Noticing how your body responds is part of stewarding it well.','Proverbs 27:23|Know well the state of your flocks, and pay attention to your herds.','Psalm 139:23; Proverbs 4:26; 1 Corinthians 6:19-20','Log today\'s gut, stress and sleep tonight; it takes two minutes.'],
 plants30:['Different plants feed different gut microbes. More variety means more diverse microbes and more of the short-chain fatty acids that protect the gut lining.','God filled creation with a remarkable variety of plants "for food" (Gen 1:29). Variety is part of His good provision.','Genesis 1:29|Behold, I have given you every herb yielding seed… and every tree… It will be your food.','Psalm 104:14-15; 1 Timothy 4:4; Daniel 1:12-15','Add two new plants to your meals today: a herb, a seed, a bean or a vegetable.'],
 guthyp:['Gut-directed relaxation retunes how the brain processes gut signals and calms gut nerves; it is one of the best-evidenced treatments for IBS.','The mind and body were made to work together. Picturing calm in the body can help the body find it.','Psalm 94:19|In the multitude of my thoughts within me, your comforts delight my soul.','Isaiah 26:3; Philippians 4:7; Psalm 116:7','Do one 15-minute gut-directed relaxation session today, lying down with a hand on your belly.'],
 gutcalm:['Responding calmly to gut sensations (after a doctor has checked for red flags) stops the fear–symptom loop and lets the gut settle.','God invites us not to be ruled by fear. Calm courage in the body is one way faith becomes physical.','Isaiah 41:10|Don\'t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don\'t be dismayed, for I am your God.','Psalm 56:3-4; 2 Timothy 1:7; John 14:27','When a gut sensation triggers fear today, name it, soften your belly and carry on with what you were doing.']
};

// ---------- render the "Why it works" box on practice pages ----------
function designBox(m){const D=PDESIGN[m.code];if(!D)return '';const [why,des,verse,more,act]=D;const [ref,txt]=verse.split('|');
 return `<div class="cols"><div class="box w12 whybox" style="--h:${m.h}"><h3><span class="k">WHY</span>Why this works</h3>${mmCard(MM_P[m.code])}
 <div class="why3"><div class="w-body"><span class="wi" aria-hidden="true">${WICON.body}</span><b>How your body responds</b><p>${esc(why)}</p></div>
 <div class="w-god"><span class="wi" aria-hidden="true">${WICON.god}</span><b>How God designed it</b><p>${esc(des)}</p></div>
 <div class="w-word"><span class="wi" aria-hidden="true">${WICON.word}</span><b>Meditate on this</b><blockquote><p>"${esc(txt)}"</p><cite>${esc(ref)}</cite></blockquote><p class="note" style="margin:6px 0 0">Also read: ${esc(more)}</p></div></div>
 <div class="actline"><b>Take action today:</b> ${esc(act)}</div></div></div>`}
const WICON={body:'<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M3 12h4l2-5 4 10 2-5h6"/></svg>',
 god:'<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 3v18M7 8h10"/></svg>',
 word:'<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h5v16H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2zM20 5a2 2 0 0 0-2-2h-5v16h5a2 2 0 0 1 2 2z"/></svg>'};

// ---------- why the struggle plans work ----------
const DWHY={
 sleep:['Writing worries down and slowing the breath lowers arousal so the brain can move into sleep; getting up when you can\'t sleep stops the bed becoming linked with frustration (CBT for insomnia, the first-choice treatment).','Sleep is a nightly act of trust. God neither slumbers nor sleeps, so you can.','Psalm 4:8|In peace I will both lay myself down and sleep, for you, LORD alone, make me live in safety.','Psalm 121:3-4; Psalm 127:2; Proverbs 3:24','Tonight, write tomorrow\'s worries on paper before bed and pray over the list.'],
 panic:['Panic is a false alarm in the body. Slow exhales and grounding switch the nervous system off alarm, and staying instead of escaping teaches the brain the sensations are safe.','God meets us in the storm, not only after it. "Peace, be still" was spoken in the middle of the waves.','Isaiah 41:10|Don\'t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don\'t be dismayed, for I am your God.','Mark 4:39-40; Psalm 34:4; 2 Timothy 1:7','Practise the physiological sigh when calm today, so it is ready for the next spike.'],
 start:['Starting tiny lowers the threat of the task. Once you begin, progress releases dopamine and momentum makes it easier to continue.','Faithfulness in small things is what God honours. You do not have to feel ready to take one step.','Ecclesiastes 11:4|He who observes the wind won\'t sow, and he who regards the clouds won\'t reap.','Zechariah 4:10; Proverbs 6:6-8; Colossians 3:23','Do two minutes of the task you are avoiding before you check your phone again.'],
 replay:['Rumination keeps the alarm running. Naming it, scheduling a set time to think and redirecting attention shrinks its hold.','God invites us to bring the conversation to Him instead of rehearsing it alone, and His peace guards the mind.','Philippians 4:6-7|Don\'t be anxious about anything, but in everything, by prayer… let your requests be made known to God. And the peace of God… will guard your hearts.','Psalm 139:23-24; Isaiah 26:3; 2 Corinthians 10:5','Write the replayed conversation in three sentences, then pray: "I hand this to You."'],
 critic:['Answering harsh self-talk with kindness activates the soothing system and makes you more, not less, likely to try again.','The accuser condemns; God convicts and restores. His voice over you is kinder than your critic.','Romans 8:1|There is therefore now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus.','Zephaniah 3:17; Isaiah 43:1; 1 John 3:20','Write down the critic\'s worst sentence, then write what God says instead.'],
 no:['Small, calm noes weaken the threat response around disappointing people. Resentment drops and relationships become more honest.','Jesus said no, withdrew and let people walk away. Your yes means more when your no is real.','Matthew 5:37|Let your "Yes" be "Yes" and your "No" be "No".','Galatians 1:10; Mark 1:35-38; Proverbs 29:25','Say one small, kind no this week without a long explanation.'],
 snap:['Snapping happens when the alarm beats the thinking brain. A pause, a breath and meeting basic needs (HALT) give the pilot time to catch up; repair afterwards restores trust.','God is slow to anger and rich in love, and He makes repair possible after we fail.','Proverbs 15:1|A gentle answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger.','James 1:19-20; Ephesians 4:26,32; Psalm 103:8','When you feel the snap coming, say "Give me two minutes" and breathe out slowly.'],
 fight:['Couples fight in cycles. Seeing the cycle as the enemy, not each other, and making a soft start-up and repair change the pattern (Gottman, EFT).','Marriage is a covenant of two becoming one. Turning toward each other in conflict mirrors Christ\'s faithful love.','Ephesians 4:2-3|With all lowliness and humility, with patience, bearing with one another in love; being eager to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace.','Colossians 3:13-14; 1 Peter 3:8; Ecclesiastes 4:12','Start your next hard conversation with "I feel… about… and I need…"'],
 numb:['Numbness is the nervous system\'s shutdown. Gentle movement, warmth, sensation and connection bring it back online slowly, without overwhelming it.','Elijah under the broom tree was given food and rest before any challenge. God is gentle with the depleted.','1 Kings 19:5-7|Arise and eat, because the journey is too great for you.','Psalm 34:18; Isaiah 42:3; Matthew 11:28','Take a five-minute walk and notice three things you can feel.'],
 scroll:['Endless feeds give unpredictable rewards, which hook the dopamine system. Adding friction and swapping in real rewards weakens the habit loop.','We were made to delight in God and in real, embodied gifts. Desire can be redirected, not just resisted.','1 Corinthians 6:12|"All things are lawful for me," but not all things are expedient… I will not be brought under the power of anything.','Psalm 16:11; Ephesians 5:15-16; Psalm 90:12','Move your most-used app off your home screen today.'],
 burnout:['Burnout is depletion, not weakness. Rest, boundaries and reconnecting with meaning restore the stress system; pushing harder deepens it.','Sabbath is God\'s design for limits. Rest is trust that the world does not depend on you.','Matthew 11:28|Come to me, all you who labour and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest.','Exodus 20:8-10; Psalm 23:2-3; Mark 6:31','Block a two-hour rest window this week and guard it like a meeting.'],
 fraud:['Imposter feelings come from linking worth to performance. Writing down evidence and talking about the feeling shrinks it.','Your worth was given before you achieved anything, like the Father\'s words over Jesus before His ministry began.','Ephesians 2:10|For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works.','Luke 3:22; 2 Corinthians 3:5; 1 Peter 4:10','Write three pieces of evidence that you belong where you are.'],
 social:['Avoidance keeps social fear alive. Going in small, planned steps and focusing outward on others teaches the brain it is safe.','We are made for fellowship, and God goes before us into every room.','Deuteronomy 31:8|The LORD himself is who goes before you. He will be with you. He will not fail you nor forsake you.','Hebrews 10:24-25; Romans 12:10; Proverbs 27:17','Plan one question to ask someone at your next gathering.'],
 lonely:['Loneliness is a signal of unmet connection, like hunger. Small, regular contact and being known by one or two people are what meet it.','God sets the lonely in families. Belonging runs through real people, not only through private faith.','Psalm 68:6|God sets the lonely in families.','Genesis 2:18; Ecclesiastes 4:9-12; John 15:15','Send one warm, no-pressure message to someone today.'],
 text:['The brain fills silence with threat. Checking the facts, delaying the follow-up and soothing yourself break the anxiety loop.','God is a constant presence when people are slow to reply. Your security does not hang on a notification.','Hebrews 13:5|I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you.','Psalm 62:1-2; Isaiah 49:15-16; Psalm 139:7-10','Put your phone in another room for 30 minutes after sending an important message.'],
 decide:['Decision paralysis comes from fear of the wrong choice. Limiting options, setting a deadline and choosing "good enough" reduces stress and regret.','God guides people who are walking, not only those who are standing still. Wisdom is available when you ask.','James 1:5|If any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all generously.','Proverbs 3:5-6; Proverbs 16:9; Isaiah 30:21','Set a decision deadline and write the two best options and one value each serves.'],
 compare:['Comparison triggers the threat and status systems. Reducing exposure and practising gratitude retrain attention toward what you have.','Each of us is crafted uniquely. Running your own race is obedience, not mediocrity.','Galatians 6:4|Let each man test his own work, and then he will take pride in himself and not in his neighbour.','Psalm 139:13-14; Hebrews 12:1-2; Philippians 4:11-12','Mute three accounts that leave you feeling worse.'],
 grief:['Grief comes in waves because the brain is relearning a world without what you lost. Letting the wave come, with support, helps it pass and integrate.','Jesus wept at His friend\'s tomb. God does not hurry grief; He draws close to it.','Psalm 34:18|The LORD is near to those who have a broken heart.','John 11:35; Matthew 5:4; Revelation 21:4','Set aside ten minutes today to remember, write or pray about the one you miss.'],
 habit:['Habits run on cues and rewards. Changing the routine while keeping the cue, and counting reps not streaks, rewires the path.','Scripture speaks of putting off the old and putting on the new, a daily replacement rather than a single fight.','Galatians 5:16|Walk by the Spirit, and you won\'t fulfill the lust of the flesh.','Ephesians 4:22-24; Romans 12:2; 1 Corinthians 10:13','Name your habit\'s cue today and choose one new response for it.'],
 eating:['Stress eating soothes the stress system for a moment. Pausing to name the real need and meeting it directly breaks the link.','Jesus is the bread of life. Our hunger for comfort points to a deeper hunger He meets.','John 6:35|I am the bread of life. Whoever comes to me will not be hungry.','Psalm 107:9; Matthew 4:4; Psalm 34:8','Before your next stress snack, ask: "What am I really hungry for?"'],
 family:['Family visits activate old emotional memories. Planning ahead, taking breaks and having a support person keep you in your window.','Honouring parents does not require surrendering your adulthood. Jesus honoured His mother and still followed His calling.','Romans 12:18|If it is possible, as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men.','Exodus 20:12; Luke 2:49; Proverbs 4:23','Plan one exit line and one break before your next family visit.'],
 dread:['Anticipatory dread is the brain rehearsing threat. A small, pleasant morning ritual and a clear first task give it something safer to expect.','His mercies are new every morning. Each day starts with grace, not with a verdict.','Lamentations 3:22-23|His compassion doesn\'t fail. They are new every morning. Great is your faithfulness.','Psalm 5:3; Psalm 118:24; Matthew 6:34','Put one thing you enjoy into tomorrow morning\'s first 15 minutes.'],
 feedback:['Feedback activates the brain\'s social pain system. Separating the useful part from the sting, and remembering your worth is not on trial, lets you learn.','Wise people welcome correction because their identity is secure in God.','Proverbs 12:1|Whoever loves correction loves knowledge.','Proverbs 15:31-32; Hebrews 12:11; Romans 8:31','Write the one useful sentence from the feedback, and let go of the rest.'],
 pullaway:['Pulling away protects against old hurt. Staying a little longer than is comfortable and naming the urge builds earned security.','Perfect love casts out fear. God moves toward us first, and teaches us to stay.','1 John 4:18|There is no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear.','Romans 5:8; Ruth 1:16; John 15:9','When you feel the urge to withdraw, say "I need a moment, and I\'ll come back", then come back.'],
 money:['Money stress feels like danger to the brain. A simple plan and one concrete action move it from the alarm to the planning brain.','God is our provider, and wise stewardship is part of trust, not the opposite of it.','Philippians 4:19|My God will supply every need of yours according to his riches in glory in Christ Jesus.','Matthew 6:31-33; Proverbs 21:5; Hebrews 13:5','Write this month\'s three biggest costs and one small saving you can make.'],
 focus:['Attention tires and gets fragmented by switching. Clear goals, single-tasking and short breaks restore it.','Mary chose the one needed thing. Undivided attention is a form of love and worship.','Luke 10:41-42|You are anxious and troubled about many things, but one thing is needed.','Proverbs 4:25; Colossians 3:23; Psalm 86:11','Work on one task for 25 minutes with notifications off.'],
 parent:['A child\'s distress sets off your own alarm. Calming yourself first lets you lend your calm to your child, which is how children learn to regulate.','God parents us with patience and repair. We pass on what we receive from Him.','Psalm 103:13|Like a father has compassion on his children, so the LORD has compassion on those who fear him.','Ephesians 6:4; Colossians 3:21; Isaiah 40:11','After your next blow-up, say: "I\'m sorry I yelled. You didn\'t deserve that. Let\'s try again."'],
 noise:['An overloaded sensory system raises stress. Lowering input and adding calming rhythm give the brain room to settle.','God spoke to Elijah in a still, small voice. Quiet is a place of meeting.','1 Kings 19:12|After the fire, there was a still small voice.','Psalm 46:10; Mark 6:31; Isaiah 30:15','Take a five-minute quiet break with low light and no screen today.'],
 talk:['Avoidance makes the conversation feel bigger. Preparing a script and choosing a time lower the threat and make a good outcome more likely.','Speaking the truth in love is how relationships grow up, not apart.','Ephesians 4:15|Speaking truth in love, we may grow up in all things into him.','Matthew 18:15; Proverbs 27:6; Colossians 4:6','Write your first sentence for the conversation and pick a day to say it.'],
 bodyday:['Body-image distress is driven by comparison and attention. Shifting focus to what your body does, and treating it kindly, reduces it.','Your body is God\'s workmanship and a temple of His Spirit, not a project to be judged.','Psalm 139:14|I will give thanks to you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.','1 Samuel 16:7; 1 Corinthians 6:19-20; 1 Peter 3:3-4','Thank God for three things your body did for you today.'],
 revenge:['Revenge keeps the offender at the centre of your attention and prolongs pain. Lament, boundaries and fair processes give the need for justice a healthier path.','God is the judge. Handing Him the sword frees you to protect yourself without being consumed.','Romans 12:19|Don\'t seek revenge yourselves… for it is written, "Vengeance belongs to me; I will repay," says the Lord.','Psalm 37:1-8; 1 Peter 2:23; Romans 12:21','Write an unsent letter, then pray it as a lament instead of sending it.'],
 sorryenough:['A wrong causes many injuries. Agreeing, asking what repair looks like and staying consistent over time rebuild trust where words alone cannot.','Zacchaeus repaired fourfold after being welcomed. Grace makes real repair possible.','Luke 19:8|If I have wrongfully exacted anything of anyone, I restore four times as much.','2 Corinthians 7:10; Matthew 5:23-24; Proverbs 28:13','Ask the person: "What would repair look like for you?" and do one part of it.'],
 parents:['Calm, clear adult decisions with warmth reduce conflict over time; guilt and pressure lose power when you stop arguing for permission.','Honour is lifelong respect and care, not unlimited obedience. God calls adults to leave and cleave.','Genesis 2:24|Therefore a man will leave his father and his mother, and will join with his wife.','Exodus 20:12; Acts 5:29; Ephesians 6:1-3','State one decision to your parents kindly and once, without asking for permission you don\'t need.'],
 shamed:['Public shame triggers the threat of losing belonging. Pausing, sorting fact from contempt and leaning on safe people protect you from reacting in shame.','Jesus was publicly shamed and did not retaliate. Your worth is not decided by a crowd.','Isaiah 54:4|Don\'t be afraid, for you will not be ashamed… you will forget the shame.','Hebrews 12:2; 1 Peter 2:23; Psalm 3:3','Put the phone down for an hour, then tell one safe person what happened.'],
 rescue:['Rescuing relieves your anxiety but keeps the other person dependent. Asking what they want and supporting their own steps builds their capacity.','Love carries others\' burdens and lets each carry their own load.','Galatians 6:2,5|Bear one another\'s burdens… For each man will bear his own burden.','Philippians 2:4; Proverbs 19:19; 2 Thessalonians 3:10','Before stepping in this week, ask: "What would you like from me?"'],
 tummy:['Slow belly breathing and a small plain snack calm the stress response so the gut can relax.','God made the body to settle when it feels safe. Your gut is not betraying you; it is protecting you.','Psalm 94:19|In the multitude of my thoughts within me, your comforts delight my soul.','Isaiah 26:3; John 14:27; Philippians 4:6-7','Before your next big moment, do six slow belly breaths with a hand on your tummy.'],
 ibsflare:['Calming the brain side (breathing, gut-directed hypnotherapy, CBT) and the gut side (regular meals, soluble fibre) breaks the stress–symptom loop.','You are fearfully made, body and mind together. Caring for both is good stewardship.','3 John 1:2|I pray that you may prosper in all things and be healthy, even as your soul prospers.','Psalm 139:14; 1 Corinthians 6:19-20; Isaiah 41:10','Do one gut-directed relaxation session this week and log how you feel after.'],
 stresseat:['Regular meals keep blood sugar steady and reduce stress-driven cravings; naming the real need lets you meet it directly.','Jesus fed people and cared about their hunger. Food is a gift, not an enemy or a hiding place.','Psalm 107:9|For he satisfies the longing soul. He fills the hungry soul with good.','Matthew 6:11; John 6:35; 1 Corinthians 10:31','Eat breakfast and lunch tomorrow, sitting down, before evening comes.'],
 bloat:['Eating slowly, walking after meals and adjusting fibre gradually reduce swallowed air and fermentation.','Rest and digest is God\'s design. A calm table helps the body do its work.','Ecclesiastes 9:7|Go your way: eat your bread with joy.','Acts 2:46; Psalm 104:14-15; 1 Timothy 4:4-5','Put your fork down between bites at one meal today.']};
function dwhyBox(m){const D=DWHY[m.sid];if(!D)return '';const [why,des,verse,more,act]=D;const [ref,txt]=verse.split('|');
 return `<div class="cols"><div class="box w12 whybox" style="--h:${m.h}"><h3><span class="k">WHY</span>Why these steps work</h3>${mmCard(MM_D[m.sid])}
 <div class="why3"><div class="w-body"><span class="wi" aria-hidden="true">${WICON.body}</span><b>What happens in you</b><p>${esc(why)}</p></div>
 <div class="w-god"><span class="wi" aria-hidden="true">${WICON.god}</span><b>How God designed it</b><p>${esc(des)}</p></div>
 <div class="w-word"><span class="wi" aria-hidden="true">${WICON.word}</span><b>Meditate on this</b><blockquote><p>"${esc(txt)}"</p><cite>${esc(ref)}</cite></blockquote><p class="note" style="margin:6px 0 0">Also read: ${esc(more)}</p></div></div>
 <div class="actline"><b>Take action today:</b> ${esc(act)}</div></div></div>`}
